    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d622">

                <pb facs="0643" n="543" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg543"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/1693: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The President of the Technical Board</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Stevens</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Harbin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-09-04T16:00:00+08:26"><hi rendition="#italic">September 4,
                                1920—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received September 5—2:30
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Apparently reliable information more than 3,000 Japanese soldiers, civilian
                    clothes, remained in Trans-Baikal distributed in small bands in the villages
                    away from the railway; arms hidden. I think 12,000 came out. There are 2,400 in
                    uniform in Harbin and many in civilian clothes and average 50 [peaceful] young
                    men looking like soldiers arrive every day. I have no doubt there are at least
                    6,000 Japanese soldiers Harbin. Japanese advise the railway officials that the
                    600 railway men will remain here indefinitely. My cipher telegram July 19, 10
                        a.m.<anchor n="69" xml:id="d622-f1"/> did not mean that I would remain here
                    indefinitely waiting for negotiations to be concluded; I am much disconcerted by
                    the failure to receive any information that [something definite] is
                    imminent.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stevens</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
